purpose: XML: the well-formedness parse, the namespace bindings it produces, and the serialization back out.

# Its own module rather than a corner of `src/html`, and the reason is the one
# ADR 0012 keeps making: the two are not the same algorithm and must never
# become one. HTML tree construction *recovers* -- every error has a defined
# repair and a document always comes out. XML **refuses**: a well-formedness
# error is fatal, and the whole of what `DOMParser.parseFromString(s,
# "text/xml")` produces for a bad string is a document containing one
# `parsererror` element. A parser that shared code with the HTML tree builder
# would have to be talked out of recovering at every step, and the first time
# somebody forgot, a page would get a tree no other browser produces.
#
# `allow: util dom` and deliberately not `url` or `html`: this module turns
# bytes into a `dom::Document` and a `dom::Node` back into bytes, and it
# resolves nothing, fetches nothing and knows no element semantics.
allow: util dom

public: XmlParser.h XmlSerializer.h

extern:

# XmlParser.cpp is the whole of XML's grammar in one pass, and splitting it
# would put the prolog in one file and content in another with the sticky error
# flag and the namespace scope stack shared between them -- two files that can
# only be read together. 1000 is what it takes; the number is here so that
# growing past it is a decision.
max_tu_lines: 1000
